// A priority scheduler with no heap: the same machine as `guide/scheduler`, built out of a fixed
// table and index numbers instead of `&T` and a run-time allocator.
//
// The axis this owns is **a program that allocates nothing**. Every task, every lock, every list
// and the trace of the run all live in one struct that the checks declare as an ordinary local, so
// the whole machine is a few tens of kilobytes of storage decided at compile time. Nothing below
// makes a `&T`, a `Buf`, or a `string` — `schedule` is the single exception, and it is the seam
// where the machine hands its answer to the reporting.
//
// Checked rather than asserted: in the emitted code, no function of `Kernel` or `RunQueue` calls
// an allocator, and the only one that touches the reference counts beyond a retain of a null owner
// is `schedule`. The retains that do appear are the price of handing the task table to the queue
// as a slice — a slice carries an owner word, and a slice of a fixed array carries a null one.
// The `no alloc` clause of `13 §4` would say all this in the source instead, and this program is
// where it does not fit: a clause is a property of the **module**, both files here are the root
// module, and the checks next door build a string on nearly every line. `schedule` is the same seam
// one file further in — it validates the trace into a `string`, which is an allocation, so even the
// machine's own file would be refused. `guide/bytecode` carries the clause instead, and the
// difference between the two is only that its machine already lived in a module of its own.
//
// It is written to be **compared**, not just to work: the shared scenarios produce byte-identical
// schedules to `guide/scheduler`'s, so the two programs check each other, and the difference
// between them is a measurement of what references were buying.
//
// What the difference turned out to be, in the order it bit:
//
//   * **An index carries nothing, so the table travels with it.** `&Task` was a task; `TaskId` is
//     a number that means something only beside `tasks`. So `RunQueue`'s every method takes the
//     table as a parameter, and the reason is not that it wants to look at the tasks — it is that
//     the queue's own links live inside them. A data structure that owns no storage cannot be
//     asked a question on its own.
//   * **There was no local name for the task being worked on, and this program is what asked for
//     one.** `var t = self.tasks[i]` binds a *copy*, so every read and every write repeated the path
//     from the table: thirteen occurrences of `self.tasks[i]` in `advance` alone, where the
//     scheduler next door wrote `t` once. `&self.tasks[i]` would have given the name back as a
//     `*Task` — the mode this program exists to do without, and one that drops the bounds and
//     `within` checking that made the indices worth having in the first place.
//
//     Answered: `ref t = self.tasks[i]` (`03 § ref`) names the place rather than copying it, and the
//     eighty-three paths through the two tables are forty-three. The walk is made once, so the
//     subscript is bounds-checked at the binding instead of at each use, and the name stays inside
//     every check a written-out path gets — which is the half a pointer could not have.
//
//     What it costs here is nothing at all, and that is a property of this program rather than of
//     the feature: the rule holding storage still while a ref stands on it asks only about steps that
//     **own** what they point at, and a fixed table reached through a `*Kernel` receiver has none. A
//     `[]T` field would have made `self.grow()` under a live ref a refusal, which is exactly what
//     `guide/hashmap` runs into next door.
//   * **A subtype bounds the slot but cannot spell "no slot".** `TaskId` is `u8 within 0..<200`,
//     so a number that is not a task cannot be made into one and every table index is in range by
//     construction. What it cannot do is carry the sentinel a kernel would have used — 255 is not
//     a `TaskId` — so an empty link is `Option[TaskId]`, a tag beside the byte. The tag is the
//     honest version and the checking is worth the byte; it is just not the representation the
//     technique is famous for.
//   * **Three bounded identities cannot be confused, and that is the payoff.** A task number, a
//     lock number and a priority level are three `u8`s with three ranges, and because `within`
//     subtypes are `new` they are three *types*. Handing a lock number to something expecting a
//     task is the bug a table-driven kernel actually makes, and here it does not compile.
//   * **An array bound and a `within` bound name the same `const`.** `[max_tasks]Task` sizes the
//     table and `within 0..<max_tasks` bounds what may index it, so the size of the table is one
//     fact written once and the two cannot drift apart. A bound is a constant *expression* — the
//     same folding an array bound and an enum discriminant go through — so `within 0..<max_tasks`
//     is as ordinary as the array beside it, and the last magic number is gone.
//   * **A name is a string and a string is allocated**, so a task and a lock are each identified
//     by the single letter they write into the trace. That is a fair trade for a scheduler and it
//     is worth knowing it *is* a trade: the faults below name a lock by its letter where the
//     scheduler next door named it by a word.
//   * **A method on a table entry copies the entry.** A receiver is a value and there is no
//     reference to take, so `self.tasks[i].is_ready()` would copy a task with its whole program in
//     it. The four predicates are therefore free functions over `State`. For the same reason every
//     method of `Kernel` takes `*self`, including the ones that only read: the machine is the
//     struct, and a by-value receiver would copy it.
//   * **Storage that is never allocated is never freed either.** The one thing that gives anything
//     back in a program like this is a frame at `return`, which is why every section of the checks
//     is a function rather than a run of statements — thirty machines standing in one frame
//     overflows the stack before the first check. Next door thirty schedulers cost thirty handfuls
//     of words, because what they held was on the heap and went away as it was dropped.
//   * **A fixed table is where a data enum's layout stops being an implementation detail.** `09`
//     specifies storage "sized for the largest variant plus a tag"; the compiler was emitting the
//     tag followed by every variant's payload side by side, so a `Step` was 20 bytes rather than 8
//     and a task's program two and a half times the size it should be. A program that puts its
//     enums on the heap one at a time never notices; two hundred of them in a table does. Fixed in
//     the compiler — the payload is one region now — and this program is what asked for it.
//   * **A struct big enough to hold the whole machine is a struct the optimizer cannot afford to
//     treat as a value.** `Kernel` is twenty kilobytes, and it was being built as a chain of
//     `insertvalue` over multi-kilobyte SSA values, returned as one, copied as one, and read a
//     field at a time by producing the whole thing first. Every one of those is free for a struct
//     of two integers, and none of them stays linear at this size: this module took **408 seconds**
//     to compile at `-O1`, which made it the critical path of the entire test suite and the reason
//     parallelising the suite bought nothing at all. Fixed in the compiler — an aggregate over 128
//     bytes is now built where it is going to live, copied with `memcpy`, read at its address,
//     returned through an out-pointer and passed as one — and the same module compiles in **0.93
//     seconds**. Nothing here changed, and nothing here could have: the shape that found it is a
//     fixed table, which is the axis this program was written on.
//   * **A slice of an array reached through a pointer was refused**, which stopped the machine from
//     handing out a view of its own trace. The escape analysis asks whether an array is one *this
//     frame owns*, and the walk that answered stopped at the dereference instead of following the
//     place to its root — so a field of somebody else's struct counted as a local. Fixed in the
//     compiler, which is this program's one change to it rather than to itself.
//
// Deliberately not modelled, exactly as next door, so the traces stay computable on paper: more
// than one processor, preemption inside a tick, and inheritance through a chain of holders.

import sysl.text.from_utf8

// The three bounded identities. Each is `new`, so none of them converts to another by accident,
// and each is checked at the moment a number becomes one.
const max_tasks: usize = 200
const max_locks: usize = 4
const max_levels: usize = 64

type TaskId = new u8 within 0..<max_tasks
type LockId = new u8 within 0..<max_locks
type Level = new u8 within 0..<max_levels

const max_steps: usize = 8
const max_ticks: usize = 512

// One thing a task does. `Work` is the only step that costs time, which is what makes the length
// of a run the sum of the work in it plus whatever the processor stood idle.
enum Step
    Work(ticks: int)
    Lock(m: LockId)
    Unlock(m: LockId)
    Sleep(ticks: int)
end Step

enum State
    Fresh
    Ready
    Blocked
    Sleeping
    Done
    Faulted
end State

is_fresh(s: State) -> bool = s match
    Fresh -> true
    _ -> false

is_ready(s: State) -> bool = s match
    Ready -> true
    _ -> false

settled(s: State) -> bool = s match
    Done -> true
    Faulted -> true
    _ -> false

// Whether some future tick could still see this task run. A blocked task is not waiting for time
// to pass, it is waiting for another task to act, so it does not count.
movable(s: State) -> bool = s match
    Fresh -> true
    Ready -> true
    Sleeping -> true
    _ -> false

// What a task can get wrong. The lock is named by its letter rather than by a word, because a
// word would be a string and a string would be allocated.
enum Fault
    NotHeld(lock: u8)
    AlreadyHeld(lock: u8)
end Fault

enum Outcome
    Running
    Finished(at: int)
    Stalled(at: int)
    Timeout(at: int)
end Outcome

// A task control block: nineteen fields, four of which exist only because there is no heap. The
// scheduler next door had fifteen and reached the same tasks through references; `next_ready`,
// `next_wait`, `next_sleep` and `holds` are the three lists and the one chain that a `&Buf` used
// to hold, moved inside the thing being listed.
struct Task
    mark: u8
    base: int
    prio: int
    arrive: int
    steps: [max_steps]Step
    nsteps: u8
    pc: u8
    did: int
    ran: int
    seq: int
    qlevel: Option[Level]
    state: State
    wake: int
    holds: Option[LockId]
    blocked_on: Option[LockId]
    fault: Option[Fault]
    next_ready: Option[TaskId]
    next_wait: Option[TaskId]
    next_sleep: Option[TaskId]
end Task

// What every slot of the table holds before anybody spawns into it. A fixed table has to be filled
// with something, and a task that has never existed is the same shape as one that has.
blank_task() -> Task
    Task(
        0u8, 0, 0, 0, [Work(0); max_steps], 0u8, 0u8, 0, 0, 0, None, Fresh, 0, None, None, None,
        None, None, None,
    )
end blank_task

// A lock. `owner` and the owner's `holds` chain are the same fact written from both ends, which is
// what lets a release know whose priority to hand back without going looking for it.
struct Mutex
    mark: u8
    owner: Option[TaskId]
    waiters: Option[TaskId]
    next_held: Option[LockId]
end Mutex

blank_mutex() -> Mutex = Mutex(0u8, None, None, None)

// The machine, and all of its storage. Everything a run needs is in here, sized at compile time:
// the two tables, the ready queue's heads and tails, the sleeper list, and the trace.
struct Kernel
    tasks: [max_tasks]Task
    locks: [max_locks]Mutex
    ntasks: u8
    nlocks: u8
    ready: RunQueue
    sleep_head: Option[TaskId]
    sleep_tail: Option[TaskId]
    now: int
    seq: int
    inherit: bool
    trace: [max_ticks]u8
    ntrace: usize
    idle: int
    overflowed: bool

    // Taking a slot out of the table. The table is the resource, so running out of it is an
    // ordinary answer rather than a failure of the machine — and so is a priority that would not
    // fit the bitmap, which is the other fixed size a caller can exceed.
    spawn(*self, mark: u8, prio: int, arrive: int, steps: []Step) -> Option[TaskId]
        if usize(self.ntasks) >= max_tasks then return None
        if prio < 0 || prio >= int(max_levels) then return None
        if steps.len > max_steps then return None

        var id = TaskId(self.ntasks)
        var i = usize(id)

        self.tasks[i] = blank_task()

        ref t = self.tasks[i]

        t.mark = mark
        t.base = prio
        t.prio = prio
        t.arrive = arrive
        t.nsteps = u8(steps.len)

        for k in 0..<steps.len do t.steps[k] = steps[k]

        self.ntasks += 1u8
        Some(id)
    end spawn

    declare(*self, mark: u8) -> Option[LockId]
        if usize(self.nlocks) >= max_locks then return None

        var m = LockId(self.nlocks)

        self.locks[usize(m)] = blank_mutex()
        ref k = self.locks[usize(m)]

        k.mark = mark
        self.nlocks += 1u8
        Some(m)
    end declare

    // Becoming ready stamps the task with the moment it did. The queue no longer compares those
    // numbers — the level's list is already in that order — but a wait list still does, and that
    // is the one place left where two ready moments have to be told apart.
    make_ready(*self, id: TaskId)
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]

        t.state = Ready
        self.seq += 1
        t.seq = self.seq
        self.ready.push(self.tasks[..], id)
    end make_ready

    key(*self, id: TaskId) -> Urgency
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]

        Urgency(t.prio, t.seq)
    end key

    before(*self, a: TaskId, b: TaskId) -> bool = self.key(a) < self.key(b)

    // What a task's priority should be right now: its own, unless it is holding a lock somebody
    // more urgent is waiting for. Two nested walks of intrusive lists where the scheduler next
    // door wrote two `for` loops over `Buf`s — the same shape, spelled through links.
    urgency(*self, id: TaskId) -> int
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]
        var p = t.base
        var m = t.holds

        while m.is_some()
            ref k = self.locks[usize(m.unwrap())]
            var w = k.waiters

            while w.is_some()
                ref waiter = self.tasks[usize(w.unwrap())]

                if waiter.prio < p then p = waiter.prio

                w = waiter.next_wait

            m = k.next_held

        p
    end urgency

    // Give a task the priority the graph says it should have, and move it in the queue if it is in
    // one. The only writer of `prio`, which is what makes "a lent priority is always given back" a
    // property of one function rather than of every call site.
    reprice(*self, id: TaskId)
        if !self.inherit then return

        var p = self.urgency(id)

        if p != self.tasks[usize(id)].prio then self.ready.reprio(self.tasks[..], id, p)
    end reprice

    // The waiter a lock should be handed to. A wait list is short and is searched rather than kept
    // in order, which is the trade a kernel makes too — and here it is the only search left, since
    // the ready queue answers the same question with a bit scan.
    most_urgent(*self, m: LockId) -> Option[TaskId]
        var best: Option[TaskId] = None
        var w = self.locks[usize(m)].waiters

        while w.is_some()
            var id = w.unwrap()

            best match
                None -> best = Some(id)
                Some(b) -> if self.before(id, b) then best = Some(id)

            w = self.tasks[usize(id)].next_wait

        best
    end most_urgent

    // Unlink a lock from its owner's chain of held locks.
    unhold(*self, id: TaskId, m: LockId)
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]
        ref k = self.locks[usize(m)]
        var cur = t.holds
        var prev: Option[LockId] = None

        while cur.is_some() && cur.unwrap() != m
            prev = cur
            cur = self.locks[usize(cur.unwrap())].next_held

        if cur.is_none() then return

        var nxt = k.next_held

        prev match
            None -> t.holds = nxt
            Some(p) -> self.locks[usize(p)].next_held = nxt

        k.next_held = None
    end unhold

    // Unlink a task from a lock's wait list.
    unwait(*self, m: LockId, id: TaskId)
        ref k = self.locks[usize(m)]
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]
        var cur = k.waiters
        var prev: Option[TaskId] = None

        while cur.is_some() && cur.unwrap() != id
            prev = cur
            cur = self.tasks[usize(cur.unwrap())].next_wait

        if cur.is_none() then return

        var nxt = t.next_wait

        prev match
            None -> k.waiters = nxt
            Some(q) -> self.tasks[usize(q)].next_wait = nxt

        t.next_wait = None
    end unwait

    // Taking a lock. Free, and it is yours; held by somebody else, and you join its wait list and
    // lend it your urgency; held by you already, and that is a mistake the machine names.
    //
    // Joining the wait list at the head costs nothing and orders nothing, which is safe here for a
    // reason worth stating: `seq` is stamped afresh on every entry to the ready queue, so no two
    // waiters ever share a key and the search below has a single answer whatever order it walks.
    acquire(*self, id: TaskId, m: LockId) -> bool
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]
        ref k = self.locks[usize(m)]

        var held = k.owner match
            Some(o) -> true
            None -> false

        if !held
            k.owner = Some(id)
            k.next_held = t.holds
            t.holds = Some(m)
            return true

        var o = k.owner.unwrap()

        if o == id
            t.fault = Some(AlreadyHeld(k.mark))
            t.state = Faulted
            return false

        t.state = Blocked
        t.blocked_on = Some(m)
        t.next_wait = k.waiters
        k.waiters = Some(id)
        self.reprice(o)
        false
    end acquire

    // Letting go. The lock is handed straight to the most urgent waiter rather than thrown open
    // for whoever runs next, and that waiter's program counter moves past the `Lock` step it
    // blocked on — it did not take the lock, it was given it.
    release(*self, id: TaskId, m: LockId) -> bool
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]
        ref k = self.locks[usize(m)]

        var mine = k.owner match
            Some(o) -> o == id
            None -> false

        if !mine
            t.fault = Some(NotHeld(k.mark))
            t.state = Faulted
            return false

        self.unhold(id, m)
        k.owner = None

        var next = self.most_urgent(m)

        if next.is_some()
            var w = next.unwrap()

            self.unwait(m, w)

            ref heir = self.tasks[usize(w)]

            heir.blocked_on = None
            heir.pc += 1u8
            k.owner = Some(w)
            k.next_held = heir.holds
            heir.holds = Some(m)
            self.make_ready(w)
            self.reprice(w)

        self.reprice(id)
        true
    end release

    // Run the task's program until it is sitting on a step that costs time, or until it can go no
    // further. The answer is whether it can spend the tick it was picked for.
    advance(*self, id: TaskId) -> bool
        ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]

        loop
            if t.pc >= t.nsteps
                t.state = Done
                return false

            t.steps[usize(t.pc)] match
                Work(n) ->
                    if t.did < n then return true

                    t.did = 0
                    t.pc += 1u8
                Sleep(n) ->
                    t.pc += 1u8
                    t.state = Sleeping
                    t.wake = self.now + n
                    self.sleep(id)
                    return false
                Lock(m) ->
                    if !self.acquire(id, m) then return false

                    t.pc += 1u8
                Unlock(m) ->
                    if !self.release(id, m) then return false

                    t.pc += 1u8
    end advance

    // Join the sleeper list at its tail, so the clock reaches sleepers in the order they lay down.
    sleep(*self, id: TaskId)
        self.tasks[usize(id)].next_sleep = None

        self.sleep_tail match
            None -> self.sleep_head = Some(id)
            Some(last) -> self.tasks[usize(last)].next_sleep = Some(id)

        self.sleep_tail = Some(id)
    end sleep

    // Arrivals and wakeups, both of which are "the clock reached the number this task was waiting
    // for". The sleepers are relinked rather than compacted: the survivors are strung back
    // together as the list is walked, which is the intrusive answer to what a `Buf` did by
    // shifting its elements down.
    admit(*self)
        for i in 0..<usize(self.ntasks)
            if is_fresh(self.tasks[i].state) && self.tasks[i].arrive <= self.now
                self.make_ready(TaskId(u8(i)))

        var cur = self.sleep_head
        var head: Option[TaskId] = None
        var tail: Option[TaskId] = None

        self.sleep_head = None
        self.sleep_tail = None

        while cur.is_some()
            var id = cur.unwrap()
            ref t = self.tasks[usize(id)]
            var nxt = t.next_sleep

            t.next_sleep = None

            if t.wake <= self.now then self.make_ready(id)
            else
                tail match
                    None -> head = Some(id)
                    Some(t) -> self.tasks[usize(t)].next_sleep = Some(id)

                tail = Some(id)

            cur = nxt

        self.sleep_head = head
        self.sleep_tail = tail
    end admit

    // The trace has a fixed length like everything else, so a run longer than it can hold says so
    // rather than quietly writing a shorter schedule than it lived.
    mark(*self, c: u8)
        if self.ntrace >= max_ticks then self.overflowed = true
        else
            self.trace[self.ntrace] = c
            self.ntrace += 1usize
    end mark

    // One tick of the machine: admit whoever the clock has reached, then hand the tick to the most
    // urgent ready task. A task that turns out not to be able to use it is out of the queue by
    // then, so the next pick is somebody else.
    tick(*self)
        self.admit()

        var spent = false

        loop
            if spent then break

            self.ready.pop(self.tasks[..]) match
                None -> break
                Some(id) ->
                    if self.advance(id)
                        var i = usize(id)

                        self.mark(self.tasks[i].mark)
                        self.tasks[i].did += 1
                        self.tasks[i].ran += 1
                        spent = true

                        // The same walk again, which is what rolls the counter past a step whose
                        // last tick this was and runs whatever instantaneous steps follow it — so
                        // a lock is released in the tick the work finished, not in the next one.
                        self.advance(id)

                        if is_ready(self.tasks[i].state) then self.make_ready(id)

        if !spent
            self.idle += 1
            self.mark(46u8)

        self.now += 1
    end tick

    // Where the run stands. Nothing left alive is a finish; something alive but nothing able to
    // move is a deadlock, and it is worth reporting as its own answer rather than as a run that
    // used up its budget.
    status(*self) -> Outcome
        var live = 0
        var able = 0

        for i in 0..<usize(self.ntasks)
            if !settled(self.tasks[i].state) then live += 1
            if movable(self.tasks[i].state) then able += 1

        if live == 0 then Finished(self.now)
        elif able == 0 then Stalled(self.now)
        else Running
    end status

    run(*self, limit: int) -> Outcome
        loop
            var s = self.status()
            var going = s match
                Running -> true
                _ -> false

            if !going then return s
            if self.now >= limit then return Timeout(self.now)

            self.tick()
    end run

    // The schedule, one letter per tick, with `.` for a tick nobody could use. This is the whole
    // assertion, and it is the same string the scheduler next door produces for the same run.
    schedule(*self) -> string
        var r = from_utf8(self.trace[0..<self.ntrace])

        r.unwrap_or("?")
    end schedule
end Kernel

// The machine, empty. Every one of the two hundred slots is written before anybody uses it, which
// is what a fixed table costs at startup and what it never pays again.
kernel(inherit: bool) -> Kernel
    Kernel(
        [blank_task(); max_tasks], [blank_mutex(); max_locks], 0u8, 0u8, run_queue(), None, None,
        0, 0, inherit, [0u8; max_ticks], 0usize, 0, false,
    )
end kernel
